  "content": {
    "id": "p69-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p69-q22-stem",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A company sells a product at a price of ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "p",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " dollars per item. Market research shows that when the price is set at $20, 1000 units are sold. However, for every $1 increase in price, 50 fewer units are sold.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p69-q22-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Express the number of units sold, $n$, in terms of $p$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=2000-50p$.",
          "worked": "Demand has gradient $-50$ and passes through $(p,n)=(20,1000)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}n-1000&=-50(p-20)\\\\n&=2000-50p\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p69-q22-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that the revenue $R(p)$ in dollars is given by a quadratic expression.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$R(p)=2000p-50p^2$ dollars.",
          "worked": "Revenue is price multiplied by the number of units sold.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}R(p)&=p(2000-50p)\\\\R(p)&=2000p-50p^2\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis is quadratic in $p$, with revenue measured in dollars.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p69-q22-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p69-q22-c-prompt",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Suppose the total cost to produce the items is $5000 plus $4 per unit. Write an expression for the profit ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(p)",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(p)=-50p^2+2200p-13000$ dollars.",
          "worked": "Cost is the fixed cost plus the cost per unit multiplied by the number of units sold.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}C(p)&=5000+4(2000-50p)\\\\C(p)&=13000-200p\\end{align*}$$\n\nProfit is revenue minus cost.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(p)&=R(p)-C(p)\\\\P(p)&=2000p-50p^2-(13000-200p)\\\\P(p)&=-50p^2+2200p-13000\\end{align*}$$\n\nProfit is measured in dollars.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p69-q22-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Find all price values $p$ for which the company makes a profit.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$22-4\\sqrt{14}<p<22+4\\sqrt{14}$, with $p$ in dollars.\n\nFind the break-even prices and use the profit graph, which opens downwards, to select the interval between them.\n\nIf prices are restricted to whole cents, the range is $7.04\\le p\\le36.96$ dollars.",
          "worked": "Profit requires $P(p)>0$. Find the break-even prices:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}-50p^2+2200p-13000&=0\\\\p^2-44p+260&=0\\\\p&=\\frac{44\\pm\\sqrt{44^2-4(1)(260)}}{2}\\\\p&=22\\pm4\\sqrt{14}\\end{align*}$$\n\nSketch the profit parabola through these intercepts. It opens downwards, so it lies above the price axis strictly between the roots.\n\n$$22-4\\sqrt{14}<p<22+4\\sqrt{14}.$$\n\nNonnegative price and demand give the feasible model range $0\\le p\\le40$. Both break-even prices lie within this range, so no further restriction is needed.\n\nThe break-even prices are approximately $7.03337045$ and $36.96662955$ dollars. If prices are restricted to whole cents, the first and last profitable prices are therefore $7.04$ and $36.96$ dollars, both included.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p69-q22-d-profit-sketch",
              "role": "solution",
              "format": "tikz",
              "code": "\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.24cm,y=0.25cm,line width=1pt,every node/.style={font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont,text=black}]\n\\definecolor{profitblue}{HTML}{268CFF}\n\\definecolor{profitred}{HTML}{EF6068}\n\\pgfmathsetmacro{\\leftroot}{22-4*sqrt(14)}\n\\pgfmathsetmacro{\\rightroot}{22+4*sqrt(14)}\n\\path[use as bounding box] (-10,-14) rectangle (53,22);\n\\draw[black,->] (-1,0) -- (43,0);\n\\draw[black,->] (0,-5) -- (0,14);\n\\node[anchor=north,yshift=-3pt] at (43,0) {$p$};\n\\node[anchor=south west,xshift=3pt,yshift=2pt] at (0,14) {$P(p)$};\n\\draw[black,densely dotted] (\\leftroot,0) -- (\\leftroot,-6);\n\\draw[black,densely dotted] (\\rightroot,0) -- (\\rightroot,-6);\n\\draw[profitblue,dashed,<-] plot[domain=5:\\leftroot,samples=40] (\\x,{11.2-0.05*(\\x-22)^2});\n\\draw[profitred,line width=1.5pt] plot[domain=\\leftroot:\\rightroot,samples=100] (\\x,{11.2-0.05*(\\x-22)^2});\n\\draw[profitblue,dashed,->] plot[domain=\\rightroot:39,samples=40] (\\x,{11.2-0.05*(\\x-22)^2});\n\\draw[profitred,fill=white] (\\leftroot,0) circle[radius=2.4pt];\n\\draw[profitred,fill=white] (\\rightroot,0) circle[radius=2.4pt];\n\\node[anchor=north] at (\\leftroot,-6.5) {$22-4\\sqrt{14}$};\n\\node[anchor=north] at (\\rightroot,-6.5) {$22+4\\sqrt{14}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
              "src": null,
              "widthMm": 120,
              "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
              "overlayOf": null,
              "transparent": false,
              "axes": null,
              "spec": {
                "sourcePage": 69,
                "sourceRegion": "Question 22(d), authored solution; graphical inequality method from teaching PDF pages 54–55.",
                "description": "The profit parabola opens downwards. Its positive segment is red, its negative continuations are dashed blue, and open root markers indicate excluded break-even prices. Dotted guides place the exact root annotations below the curve. Axis titles are offset from arrowheads.",
                "mathematics": "P(p)=-50p^2+2200p-13000; roots 22±4√14; vertex (22,11200); P(p)>0 strictly between the roots. Horizontal coordinates are price in dollars and vertical coordinates are profit in dollars.",
                "answerVisibility": "Solution only. No diagram or answer is supplied in the student prompt."
              },
              "derived": false,
              "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
            }
          ]
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 120
      }
    ]
